'''HMS ''Afridi''''' was a {{sclass2|Tribal|destroyer|||1905}} of the [[Royal Navy]] [[Ceremonial ship launching|launched]] in 1907 and sold for [[ship breaking|scrap]] in 1919. During the [[First World War]] she served in the [[North Sea]] and the [[English Channel]] with the [[6th Destroyer Flotilla]] and as part of the [[Dover Patrol]].

It is assumed - though never definitely confirmed - that explosive sweeps deployed by ''Afridi'' were responsible for the destruction of the German U-boat {{SMU|UB-13}}, which on 23 April 1916 departed [[Zeebrugge]] for a patrol off the mouth of the [[River Thames|Thames]] and was never heard from again.

==Construction and design==
HMS ''Afridi'' was ordered from [[Armstrong Whitworth]] in September 1905,<ref name="export p176">Brook, 1999, p. 176.</ref> one of five Tribal-class destroyers ordered for the [[Royal Navy]] as part of the 1905–06 shipbuilding programme.<ref name="friedman p108">Friedman 2009, p. 108.</ref> Design of the ships was left to the builders, with the [[British Admiralty|Admiralty]] only laying down loose requirements, although the builder's designs did need to be approved by the [[Director of Naval Construction]] before orders were placed. The destroyers were required to reach {{convert|33|kn|lk=in}}, and have a range of {{convert|1500|nmi|lk=in}} at cruising speed and an endurance of eight hours at full speed. The ships were required to be powered by [[steam turbine]]s, with cruising turbines fitted to improve range, fed by oil-fired boilers. [[Freeboard (nautical)|Freeboard]] was to be at least {{convert|15|ft|6|in|m}} at the ship's [[Bow (ship)|bow]]. Armament was two be three 12-pounder (3-inch, 76&nbsp;mm) guns, with the older [[QF 12-pounder 12 cwt naval gun|12 cwt gun]] chosen to save money,{{#tag:ref|cwt stands for [[hundredweight]], 12 cwt referring to the weight of the gun.|group=lower-alpha}} and two 18 in (450&nbsp;mm) [[torpedo tube]]s.<ref name="friedman p106-8">Friedman 2009, pp. 106–108.</ref><ref name="conways06 p72">Gardiner and Gray 1985, p. 72.</ref> It was realised during construction that the armament of the Tribals was too light, with the two ships ordered as part of the 1906–07 programme being armed with two 4-inch (102&nbsp;mm) guns. In October 1908, it was decided to strengthen the armament of the first five Tribals, including ''Afridi'', by adding another two 12-pounder guns. This was done to ''Afridi'' prior to acceptance by the Royal Navy.<ref name="friedman p109-0">Friedman 2009, pp. 109–110.</ref>

Armstrong's design used five [[Yarrow boiler|Yarrow]] [[water-tube boiler]]s to feed steam turbines supplied by the [[Parsons Marine Steam Turbine Company]] rated at {{convert|14250|shp|kW|abbr=on|lk=in}}. These drove three [[propeller]] shafts, with one propeller per shaft. Three short [[Funnel (ship)|funnels]] were fitted. The two forward 12-pounder guns were mounted side-by-side on the ship's [[forecastle]], with the third gun aft. Two spare [[torpedo]]es were carried.<ref name="export p175">Brook 1999, p. 175.</ref><ref name="friedman p100">Friedman 2009, p. 100.</ref><ref name="eng51007 p480">{{cite magazine|title=H.M. Torpedo Boat Destroyer Afridi|magazine=[[The Engineer (UK magazine)|The Engineer]]|date=10 May 1907|volume=103|page=480|url=http://www.gracesguide.co.uk/images/8/8c/Er19070510a.pdf}}</ref>

''Afridi'' was [[Keel laying|laid down]] at Armstrong's [[Elswick, Tyne and Wear|Elswick]] shipyard in August 1906{{#tag:ref|Sources differ as to the date of the ship being laid down, with Friedman stating 9 August 1906<ref name="friedman p305">Friedman 2009, p. 305.</ref> while Brook states 10 August.<ref name="export p175"/>|group=lower-alpha}} and was [[Ceremonial ship launching|launched]] on 8 May 1907.<ref name="export p175"/><ref name="friedman p305"/> The ship's [[Sea trial|trials]] were troublesome, with propeller and turbine problems being encountered, with poor weather and industrial action causing delays. She finally met her contract speed during February 1909, when a speed of {{convert|33.25|kn}} was reached,<ref name="export p176"/> although this required forcing the ship's engines to give {{convert|21000|shp|abbr=on}}.<ref name="conways06 p72"/>

==Service==
''Afridi'' was [[Ship commissioning|commissioned]] on 10 September 1909, 26 months after the contracted date, the last destroyer to be built by Armstrongs,<ref name="export p175"/> joining the [[1st Destroyer Flotilla]].<ref name="nmm">{{cite web|url=http://www.rmg.co.uk/upload/pdf/Warship_Histories_Vessels_v.pdf|title=NMM, vessel ID 379493|work=Warship Histories, vol v|publisher=[[National Maritime Museum]]|accessdate=16 August 2013|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20131030115558/http://www.rmg.co.uk/upload/pdf/Warship_Histories_Vessels_v.pdf|archivedate=30 October 2013}}</ref> By 1913, ''Afridi'' had joined the [[4th Destroyer Flotilla]],<ref name="nmm"/> based at [[HMNB Portsmouth|Portsmouth]].<ref name="manning p25">Manning 1961, p. 25.</ref> In October that year, the Tribals were officially designated the F class, and as such the letter "F" was painted on ''Afridi''{{'}}s bow.<ref name="conways06 p72"/><ref name="friedman p100"/>

In February 1914, the Tribals, whose range was too short for effective open sea operations, were sent to [[Dover]], forming the [[6th Destroyer Flotilla]].<ref name="conways06 p72"/> On the outbreak of the [[First World War]], the 6th Flotilla formed the basis of the [[Dover Patrol]],<ref name="friedman p139">Friedman 2009, p. 139.</ref> with which the Tribal class, including ''Afridi'', served for the duration of the war.<ref name="wi7 p22-3">Burt 1986, pp. 22–23.</ref> Early in the war, the main means of detecting submerged submarines was the [[indicator net]], in which a submarine would get caught up in the net, with movement of an attached [[buoy]] indicating the location of the submarine.<ref>Naval Staff Monograph No. 29 1925, pp. 33–34.</ref> On 20 February 1915, the destroyer {{HMS|Viking|1909|2}} spotted a disturbance in indicator nets in the Dover Straits, and signalled to ''Afridi'' to deploy her explosive anti-submarine [[Paravane (weapon)|sweep]], but no submarine was found.<ref>Naval Staff Monograph No. 29 1925, p. 75.</ref> On 6 April 1915, the drifter ''Hyacinth'' spotted a periscope of the German submarine {{SMU|U-33|Germany|6}} and called up ''Afridi'', which was nearby. While the submarine carried away a section of the netting, the indicator buoys did not deploy, so ''U-33'' was able to escape unscathed.<ref>Naval Staff Monograph No. 29 1925, pp. 161–162.</ref>

On 24 March 1916, the cross-Channel ferry {{SS|Sussex||2}} was torpedoed by the German submarine {{SMU|UB-29}}. ''Afridi'' was one of a number of destroyers sent to respond to distress signals from the damaged ferry, and helped in the rescue operations.<ref>{{cite web|last=Corbett|first=Julian S.|title=History of the Great War: Naval Operations Vol. III| publisher=Naval-History.net|year=2013|orig-year=Originally published by Longmans, Green and Co.: London, 1921|url=http://www.naval-history.net/WW1Book-RN3a.htm|accessdate=14 December 2013}}</ref>

In order to counter German destroyers armed with 105&nbsp;mm guns, which outgunned British destroyers of the Dover patrol, a number of the Tribal class were rearmed. ''Afridi'' had her 12-pounder guns replaced by two [[QF 4.7-inch Gun Mk I–IV|4.7-inch (120 mm) QF gun]]s between April and October 1917, also receiving a single [[QF 2-pounder naval gun|2-pounder "pom-pom"]] autocannon anti-aircraft gun and a [[Maxim machine gun]].<ref name="conways06 p72"/><ref name="friedman p100"/><ref>{{cite web|last=DiGuilian|first=Tony|title=Britain: 4.7"/40 (12 cm) Elswick: 4.7"/40 (12 cm) QF Marks I, II, III, IV and VI: Italy: 120 mm/40 (4.7") Models 1889, 1891 and 1893: Japan: 4.7"/40 (12 cm) Elswick: 4.7"/40 (12 cm) 41st Year Type: 12 cm/40 (4.7") 41st Year Type|url= http://www.navweaps.com/Weapons/WNBR_47-40_mk1.htm |work=navweaps.com|date=23 September 2012|accessdate=14 December 2013}}</ref>

On 22–23 April, ''Afridi'' took part in an [[First Ostend Raid|attack]] on the German-held port of [[Ostend]] in Belgium, which took place at the same time as a similar [[Zeebrugge Raid|attack on Zeebrugge]], with the objective of denying the use of these ports for German surface vessels and submarines. ''Afridi'' formed part of the escort for the [[blockship]]s {{HMS|Sirius|1892|6}} and {{HMS|Brilliant|1891|6}}, which were to be scuttled in the harbour entrance, while [[Monitor (warship)|monitors]] bombarded the port with heavy guns.<ref>{{London Gazette|issue=31189|date=18 February 1919|pages=2519–2521|supp=y}}</ref><ref name="navopVpt2">{{cite web|last=Newbolt|first=Henry|title=History of the Great War: Naval Operations Vol. V| publisher=Naval-History.net|year=2013|orig-year=Originally published by Longmans, Green and Co.: London, 1931|url=http://www.naval-history.net/WW1Book-RN5b.htm|accessdate=14 December 2013}}</ref> While the attack on Zeebrugge was a partial success, the Ostend part of the operation was a failure, as the blockships missed the entry to the harbour and run aground, so that the port was not blocked.<ref name="navopVpt2"/><ref>{{London Gazette|issue=31189|date=18 February 1919|page=2532|supp=y}}</ref>

''Afridi'' along with ''Zubian'', ''Cossack'' and ''Viking'' were all offered for sale at Immingham in November 1919.<ref>"News in Brief." Times [London, England] 22 Nov. 1919: 9. The Times Digital Archive. Web. 21 Dec. 2013.</ref> ''Afridi'' was sold for [[Ship breaking|scrapping]] on 9 December 1919, and was eventually sunk as a target on 9 April 1930.<ref name="friedman p305"/>

